\description{
Builds the parameter list that controls \code{\link[=simulate_eyeris]{simulate_eyeris()}}. Every
component of the synthetic pupil time series (tonic baseline, slow drift,
hippus, task-evoked phasic responses, blinks, transient spikes, measurement
noise, and optional line noise) is independently toggle-able and
parameterized here, so a single scenario can isolate exactly the feature it
needs to teach.
}
\details{
The synthetic signal is composed additively in arbitrary units (a.u.) matched
to real EyeLink \strong{pupil diameter} data (which typically ranges from roughly
3600--7000 a.u.):

\deqn{L(t) = B_0 + \mathrm{drift}(t) + \mathrm{hippus}(t) +
  \sum_k \mathrm{phasic}_k(t)}
\deqn{\mathrm{pupil}(t) = L(t) + \sum_j \mathrm{transient}_j(t) +
  \mathrm{line}(t) + \mathrm{noise}(t)}

after which blink and dropout artifacts are stamped on (see
\code{\link[=simulate_eyeris]{simulate_eyeris()}}).

\strong{Components (each independently toggle-able):}
\itemize{
\item \strong{Tonic baseline} (\code{baseline}): a constant offset \code{baseline_mean}.
\item \strong{Linear drift} (\code{drift}): a slow tonic decline
\eqn{-\mathrm{drift\_slope} \cdot t} (a.u. per second), plus an optional
quadratic term \code{drift_curv}. This is precisely what
\code{\link[=detrend]{detrend()}} removes.
\item \strong{Hippus} (\code{hippus}): a slow arousal oscillation
\eqn{A_h \sin(2\pi f_h t + \phi)} that sits below the low-pass cutoff and
therefore survives \code{\link[=lpfilt]{lpfilt()}}.
\item \strong{Phasic response} (\code{phasic}): the canonical task-evoked pupil
response modeled with the Hoeks & Levelt (1993) Erlang gamma kernel
\eqn{h(\tau) = \tau^n e^{-n\tau / t_{max}}}, peak-normalized and scaled by
\code{phasic_amp}, time-locked to stimulus onsets.
\item \strong{Blinks} (\code{blinks}): runs of \strong{missing data (\code{NA})} flanked by the
rapid partial-occlusion down-then-up spikes that surround real blinks ---
the artifact \code{\link[=deblink]{deblink()}} is designed to remove.
\item \strong{Transients} (\code{transients}): isolated, physiologically implausible
fast spikes (tracker glitches) that \code{\link[=detransient]{detransient()}} targets.
\item \strong{Noise} (\code{noise}): broadband white measurement noise. Always keep
\code{noise_sd > 0} --- a perfectly noiseless signal makes the median absolute
deviation of the pupil speed zero, which aborts \code{\link[=detransient]{detransient()}}.
\item \strong{Line noise} (\code{line}): an optional high-frequency tone used to
demonstrate aliasing when decimating without an anti-alias filter.
}
}
